Nintendo has a successful franchise in  almost every genre for gaming but not  since Metroid Prime is it committed to  the shooter genre with as much gusto as  it is the splatoon the creatively  nonviolent shooter coming to Wii U this  may intono isn't scared of small  experiments introducing lightweight  games and new characters on occasion but  it's banking big on splatoon success in  hopes it will blossom into yet another  flagship Nintendo series by taking a  wholly wholesome approach to shooter  design splatoon already stands out as  something new and different bucking the  need for direct comparisons to bloody  behemoths such as Call of Duty and  Battlefield there are no headshots or  kill counts here in multiplayer modes  the goal is to cover the map in your  team's color using guns that are loaded  with ink rather than bullets because you  an inkling are a human squid hybrid  naturally the Inklings duel species  status means they can walk on two feet  but also swim through puddles of ink an  ability that's incredibly useful and  unlike any maneuver seen in other  shooters while swimming through the  opposing teams ink slows you down and  makes you an obvious target your own ink  is the perfect hiding place as enemies  can't see you if you don't move while in  squid form given that Inklings move  faster through ink than on their feet  you can also take advantage of this  ability to reposition yourself on the  map dodge enemy ink and get to high  ground where your modest jump ability  otherwise wouldn't allow the standard  turf war competitive matches are a blast  in splatoon and while it takes a bit to  get over the shoot-to-kill mentality for  most shooters it does sink in eventually  and that transitional moment is when  splatoon's greatness finally clicks  killing is a means to an end rather than  the end goal cover an entire area in  paint and you feel as good as you would  if you were taking out an opponent  something through inked across the map  and using it to pop in and out of cover  provides depth and strategy beyond what  many shooters offer which rarely extends  beyond weapon choice in positioning  players can also earn currency during  matches and use it to purchase new  clothing from splatoon humorous and  charming hub world which often comes  with stat boosts enjoying these  mechanics and the moments that result  from exploring their potential says  splatoon apart from other shooters but  it's missing something that every  competitive shooter must have if it's  going to be taken seriously voice chat  in Tendo for whatever reason has stated  there will not be voice chats with tune  rather than strategize by direct  communication Nintendo suggests that  players use the bird's-eye view of the  map on the gamepad to determine their  strategy but that's honestly a poor  replacement for the real deal  Nintendo has been pitching splatoon as a  competitive game first and foremost but  there is a single  player adventure inside that's fun on  its own offering a unique set of  equipment challenges and most  importantly level designs here you  actually are fighting to kill basically  taking out the octarians your  octopus-like adversaries you explore  original single-player only maps that  include squid and ink based platforming  mechanics and elements that are actually  reminiscent of mario galaxy of all  things this is a great way to hone your  skills before jumping into ranked  multiplayer matches but you'll actually  have plenty of time to practice for  those anyhow because they're only going  to be available post-launch  once a certain number of players reach  level 10 in the standard multiplayer  mode then Nintendo will set that feature  life splatoon has come a long way since  e3 where it was presented as more of a  vertical slice or a concept rather than  a complete vision now splatoon has an  established world and story a  single-player mode that feels distinct  from multiplayer matches and other  features including gear and ranked  matches that might learn fans of other  competitive shooters the lack of voice  chat is definitely concerning but still  I had a really fun time playing as a  squid trying to sneak around a map and  cover enemy territory with ink that I  actually wasn't too concerned for what  my team was doing really we were all  doing the same thing and I was more  concerned about where I could paint  rather than where my opponents were  splatoon splashes onto the Wii U in just  a couple of months and with it one of  Nintendo's biggest bets in a long time  its success both as a project and as a  multiplayer shooter but rely on a large  active player base it's not something  Nintendo's content it with in the past  and if anything it'll be interesting to  see how Nintendo handles all the new  challenges that splatoon represents  you
 

